{
  "image_name": "5ce9acd0f787c85a03dcb1d8f5896230.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how artificial nanoreactors are designed by mimicking the structure and function of natural cells, then shows how increasing reactor size and complexity can improve catalysis. At the top, a cell is translated into several synthetic compartment types, including vesicles, protein cages, liposomes, micelles, and porous materials, all presented as cell-inspired reaction spaces.\n\nThe lower part organizes these systems along a rough size scale from sub-nanometre to sub-micrometre. On the smallest end, “micropore cages” such as zeolites, MOFs, and COFs act as sub-nanometre reactors that convert starting molecules into products. At larger scales, nanometre reactors use hierarchical 1D, 2D, and 3D structures, with a catalyst enclosed while reactants enter and products leave. Beyond this, sub-micrometre reactors combine multiple materials and compartments through “multiscale recombination,” suggesting more complex internal organization.\n\nThe main message is that moving from simple porous cages to more elaborate, cell-like multiscale reactors creates useful nanoreactor effects. The highlighted benefits include shape-selective catalysis, nanoconfinement, molecular recognition, reactant enrichment, multiple interfaces and active sites, function isolation, and tandem catalysis.",
